# Live components

A live component is a piece of interactive UI whose state and logic run on
the server. The browser holds a small client that sends events, such as
clicks and key presses, up a WebSocket, and applies the changes to the page
that come back.

You write no JavaScript and no JSON API. The component can use your
database and services directly, because it is already on the server, and
the visitor cannot see or tamper with its logic. The cost is a connection
held open per visitor and a round trip for every interaction, so it suits
dashboards, forms and admin tools better than a drawing app.

For a piece of a page that needs no server, such as a counter or a drawing
surface, use an [island](/docs/ui/islands) instead: a Lustre app that runs
in the browser.

Under the hood these are [Lustre](https://hexdocs.pm/lustre) server
components. `howdy/ui/live` connects them to Howdy's
[WebSockets](/docs/controllers/websockets).

## A counter

A component is a Lustre app, made of three functions:

- `init` makes the starting state, called the model.
- `update` takes the model and a message, and returns the new model.
- `view` turns the model into elements.

```gleam
import gleam/int
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/button.{Primary, Secondary}
import lustre
import lustre/element.{text}
import lustre/event

pub type Msg {
  Increment
  Decrement
}

pub fn app() {
  lustre.simple(init, update, view)
}

fn init(start: Int) -> Int {
  start
}

fn update(count: Int, msg: Msg) -> Int {
  case msg {
    Increment -> count + 1
    Decrement -> count - 1
  }
}

fn view(count: Int) {
  ui.card([], [
    ui.h2("Counter"),
    ui.p([text(int.to_string(count))]),
    ui.row([], [
      ui.button(Secondary, [event.on_click(Decrement)], [text("-")]),
      ui.button(Primary, [event.on_click(Increment)], [text("+")]),
    ]),
  ])
}
```

Events in the view, such as `event.on_click(Increment)`, name the message
to send. When the button is clicked, the message goes to the server,
`update` runs, `view` runs with the new model, and Lustre sends the browser
only what changed.

`lustre.simple` is for components that just hold state. For one that also
needs to do things, such as load data or set a timer, use
`lustre.application`, whose `init` and `update` also return *effects*. The
Lustre documentation covers these.

## Serving and mounting

A component needs a route for its socket:

```gleam
import howdy/controller
import howdy/ui/live

controller.new("counter")
|> controller.get("/", fn(ctx) { live.serve(ctx, counter.app(), with: 0) })
```

The `with:` value is passed to `init`, here as the starting count. It is how
a handler hands the component what it needs, such as an id from the path
or the signed-in user.

Then give it a place in a [page](/docs/ui/pages):

```gleam
import howdy/ui/page

page.new("Counter")
|> page.live
|> page.body([ui.container([], [live.mount("/counter")])])
|> page.respond(ctx)
```

`page.live` adds the client script to the page, and is needed once however
many components the page has. `live.mount` takes the path of the socket
route, and marks where the component appears.

Each connection gets its own copy of the component, started when the socket
opens and shut down when it closes. Two visitors, or two tabs, have two
separate counts. A reload starts again from `init`, so anything that must
survive one belongs in your database, not only in the model.

## One component for everyone

Sometimes every visitor should see the same thing: a dashboard, a scoreboard,
a list of who is online. Start one component when your app starts, and
attach every socket to it:

```gleam
pub fn main() {
  let assert Ok(scoreboard) = live.start(scoreboard.app(), with: Nil)

  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.controller(pages(scoreboard))
  |> howdy.start
}
```

```gleam
controller.get("/scoreboard", fn(ctx) { live.serve_shared(ctx, scoreboard) })
```

`live.start` returns a *runtime* that outlives any one connection. With
`live.serve_shared`, every client sees the same model, and when one visitor
clicks a button the view changes for all of them. Closing a socket detaches
that client and leaves the runtime running.

`live.dispatch` sends a runtime a message from anywhere in your program,
which arrives at `update` just as a click would:

```gleam
fn record_goal(ctx: Context, scoreboard) {
  live.dispatch(scoreboard, scoreboard.GoalScored("home"))
  controller.status(ctx, 204)
}
```

That is how a background job, a webhook or an ordinary handler updates what
every visitor is looking at.

## Live links

Live links move between live pages without reloading the page. The page
has one *outlet* in place of a `live.mount`, and each link says which live
route to put in it:

```gleam
fn layout(ctx, mount mount: String) {
  page.new("Shop")
  |> page.live
  |> page.body([
    ui.row([], [
      live.link(to: "/", mount: "/live/home", children: [text("Home")]),
      live.link(to: "/orders", mount: "/live/orders", children: [
        text("Orders"),
      ]),
    ]),
    live.outlet(mount),
  ])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}

pub fn pages() {
  controller.new("/")
  |> controller.get("/", fn(ctx) { layout(ctx, mount: "/live/home") })
  |> controller.get("/orders", fn(ctx) { layout(ctx, mount: "/live/orders") })
}
```

`/live/home` and `/live/orders` are ordinary live routes, served with
`live.serve` as above.

When a visitor clicks a link, the outlet connects to the link's `mount`
route, and the address bar shows its `to` path. The old view stays on
screen until the new one arrives. Everything outside the outlet is left
alone, including other live components, which keep their connections. Back
and forward swap the outlet too.

**Every `to` path must serve the whole page**, with that view in the outlet,
as `/orders` does above. It is what a reload, a bookmark, a new tab or a
browser without JavaScript loads. Writing the page as one layout function
that takes the mount, as here, keeps them in step.

- A click with a modifier key, a middle click, a link with a `target`, and
  a link to another site all behave as plain links. So does every live link
  on a page with no outlet.
- A page has at most one outlet.
- Links work inside live views as well as in the page, so a view can link
  to the next one.
- `live.navigate(to:, mount:)` returns the attributes that make an `<a>` a
  live link, for a link you style yourself. `live.link` is styled as a
  Howdy UI link.
- Put `live.title("Orders")` anywhere in a view to change the document's
  title when that view mounts, so history entries are named after their
  page.
- After a click, the page scrolls to the top and focus moves to the outlet,
  so keyboard and screen reader users start at the new content.
- Each swap starts a fresh copy of the view, so its state does not carry
  across: going back to a counter starts it again from `init`. Views
  attached to a shared runtime with `live.serve_shared` show the shared
  model as usual.

`page.live` includes the script that makes links work. If you render the
document yourself, add `live.script()` after `server_component.script()`.

## Who may connect

A live route is an ordinary route that ends in a WebSocket, so everything
about [WebSockets](/docs/controllers/websockets) applies:

- It passes through [middleware](/docs/middleware/overview) and
  [guards](/docs/controllers/guards). Put it on a guarded controller to
  require a signed-in user, and pass the user to the component with `with:`.
- Browsers cannot set headers on a WebSocket handshake, so the guard must
  read a cookie or a query parameter.
- Only your own site may connect by default, including behind a proxy that
  terminates TLS. Other origins answer `403`.

Remember that messages come from the browser. A visitor can send any
message your `Msg` type allows, at any time, whether or not the button for
it is on their screen. Check permissions in `update`, not only in `view`.

To change the origin rules or add callbacks of your own, `live.socket` and
`live.socket_shared` return the socket before it is upgraded, as a
`howdy/websocket` builder:

```gleam
import howdy/websocket

live.socket(counter.app(), 0)
|> websocket.allow_origins(["https://app.example.com"])
|> websocket.upgrade(ctx)
```

## Islands in a live view

A live view can render [islands](/docs/ui/islands) with `island.mount`.
The live view draws each one once and leaves it to the browser: the
island's clicks never reach the server, and the live view's re-renders
leave its state alone. Rendering a different island in its place, or the
same one with different flags, starts a fresh one.

## Styling

A live component renders inside a *shadow root*, which keeps the page's CSS
out and the component's CSS in. Howdy UI handles this for you. The component
carries the CSS for exactly the classes its view uses, so
[components](/docs/ui/components) look the same inside it as outside,
however the page gets its own styles.

[Themes](/docs/ui/themes) do cross into the component, because they are CSS
variables, which are inherited. A live view changes colour with the rest of
the page when a visitor toggles the theme.

One consequence: CSS you write by hand in the page's stylesheet does not
reach inside a live component. Style its contents with
[components and classes](/docs/ui/custom-components) instead.

## Testing

A component is three plain functions, so test them directly, with no socket
and no browser:

```gleam
pub fn increment_adds_one_test() {
  assert counter.update(1, counter.Increment) == 2
}
```

To test what a view shows, render it to a string:

```gleam
import gleam/string
import lustre/element

pub fn view_shows_the_count_test() {
  let html = counter.view(3) |> element.to_string
  assert string.contains(html, ">3<")
}
```

Both need `update` and `view` to be public. For the route, `howdy/testing`
has no connection to upgrade, so a live route that gets as far as
connecting answers `426`. That is enough to check the guard in front of it:

```gleam
import howdy/testing

pub fn counter_needs_a_session_test() {
  let res = testing.get("/counter") |> testing.send(app())
  assert res.status == 401
}
```
